    "question": "A body of mass $2\\text{ kg}$ is dropped from rest from a height of $6\\text{ m}$ above the ground. Neglecting air resistance, its speed just before striking the ground is ($g = 10\\text{ m/s}^2$):",
    "options": {
      "A": "10.95 m/s",
      "B": "15.49 m/s",
      "C": "5.48 m/s",
      "D": "21.91 m/s"
    },
    "optionsList": [
      "10.95 m/s",
      "15.49 m/s",
      "5.48 m/s",
      "21.91 m/s"
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nBy conservation of mechanical energy, loss in gravitational potential energy equals gain in kinetic energy: $mgh = \\frac{1}{2} m v^2 \\implies v = \\sqrt{2gh}$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nHeight $h = 6\\text{ m}$, $g = 10\\text{ m/s}^2$, initial speed $u = 0$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nv = \\sqrt{2gh}\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Gravitational PE lost: Delta U = mgh = 120 J.\n2. Equating to KE: 0.5 * m * v^2 = 120 implies v^2 = 120.\n3. v = sqrt(120) = 10.95 m/s.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: 10.95 m/s\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nFor free fall from height h, speed on reaching the ground is always sqrt(2gh).",
